    ConsumeWebServiceSPID(_configuracion.StrURLWebService, strWebServiceXML)) ?? string.Empty;
The host receives a tick CancellationToken but cannot pass it through these contracts. SQL overloads select CancellationToken.None; the HTTP call blocks a pool thread, and the loop never checks shutdown cancellation.
Shared lifecycle context was verified: stop cancels the tick token, waits only a bounded time, and its wait helper can return after timeout without completing the task. Consequently the local batch can continue HTTP/SQL work after cleanup marks the service stopped and disposes the DI container. This is not a claim that the base permits overlapping timer ticks; it serializes them.
Fix: add/propagate a CancellationToken through application/repository methods, pass it to existing shared SQL overloads, and replace Task.Run-wrapped synchronous transport with a reusable .NET Framework-compatible async HTTP transport whose request and body reads are cancellable/bounded.
