The latter occurs after the nonquery result has been awaited and discarded. The current shared repository contract was followed through its overloads and validation: a caught SqlException can produce Exito=false without throwing; the query's strict validation flag defaults to false. Therefore the local query can report no pending work on SQL failure, and a failed update can be logged/returned as successful. An actual exception becomes false in the private update method, then the loop simply continues without a batch failure result.
This finding is the service's misuse of a shared result contract, not a duplicate finding against MUFG_T24 internals. Procedure-generated errors that the library already throws must not be counted as additional swallowed-error cases.
Fix: check Exito before consuming query data, capture/check the nonquery result before success logging, and preserve SQL Exception context. For example, after capturing the result:
Code block (csharp)
if (resultadoSp == null || !resultadoSp.Exito)
{
    throw new InvalidOperationException(
        "FIEL persistence failed.", resultadoSp?.Exception);
}
Apply the equivalent guard to pending/configuration reads. Do not erase a failed persistence outcome with an unconditional true, empty list, or unreported loop continuation. If per-record continuation is required, return an explicit batch outcome with failed identities; confirm retry/idempotency policy with the owner.
Benefit / tests: empty-success and failed-query are distinguishable; failure never produces a "Datos Actualizados" event. Test Exito=false with empty and populated Data, null results, SQL exceptions, and one failed record among successful records. Verify the application receives the failure and FIEL-FR-2 cleanup still runs.
