FIEL-FR-6 - Remove unredacted customer identifiers and SOAP payloads from routine logs
Category: MustChange/rewrite. Severity: Blocker. Checklist: sections 5/9.
Locations: identifier:101 (../../SPEI_SPID/SPID_FIEL/Infraestructura/Repositorio/OperacionesRepositorio.cs#L101), request XML:130 (../../SPEI_SPID/SPID_FIEL/Infraestructura/Repositorio/OperacionesRepositorio.cs#L130), response XML:143 (../../SPEI_SPID/SPID_FIEL/Infraestructura/Repositorio/OperacionesRepositorio.cs#L143), every return value:162 (../../SPEI_SPID/SPID_FIEL/Infraestructura/Repositorio/OperacionesRepositorio.cs#L162).
Current example:
Code block (csharp)
Config.EscribirLog($"INFO|ValidaFIEL|XML a Enviar: {strWebServiceXML}",
    NombreServicio.SPID_FIEL.ToString());
Four logging sites expose the raw customer FIEL value, its request envelope, the complete response, and each parsed value at INFO. This copies customer/certificate-query data into general operational logs. No masking flag is supplied at these call sites. No claim is made that the values are private keys or passwords; their exact classification, log ACLs and retention policy are unavailable.
The effective shared logging path was checked: FileLogger always applies sensitive-key redaction, even when the flag is false, but that filter handles recognized key/value text. These colon-delimited FIEL messages and SOAP element bodies are not covered by that filter. This is not a claim that MUFG_T24 has no redaction facility, nor a duplicate finding against the logger.
Fix: use allow-listed operational fields, a non-sensitive correlation ID, an approved irreversible/masked customer reference, outcome code and elapsed time. Keep payload capture disabled by default and subject to explicit approved access/retention controls if it is genuinely required.
Example replacement shape, using non-sensitive metadata rather than request/response contents:
